# Scripts that get executed by a POSIX shell (bash, or a bash-compatible
# interpreter like Git for Windows' bundled sh) must always check out with LF
# line endings, regardless of the cloning machine's core.autocrlf setting —
# a CRLF-terminated shebang or `set -euo pipefail` line fails to parse
# ("invalid option name"), breaking the script outright. Windows' commonly
# recommended `core.autocrlf=true` converts LF to CRLF on checkout by
# default; without this file, a fresh clone on such a machine silently
# breaks every .sh/.bats file the moment it's actually run.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bats text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for Python: a CRLF-corrupted shebang line breaks direct
# execution, and mixed line endings inside a file are a needless diff-noise
# and tooling risk.
*.py text eol=lf

# Git hook files (pre-commit, pre-push) have no extension but are POSIX
# shell scripts executed directly by git — same requirement.
CovenantMac/templates/pre-commit text eol=lf
CovenantMac/templates/pre-push text eol=lf

# Everything else: let git normalize line endings for text diffing, but
# don't force a specific ending — these aren't executed as scripts.
*.md text
*.json text
*.yml text
*.yaml text
*.cjs text
*.pylintrc text

# PowerShell scripts are meant to run on Windows; leave their line endings
# alone rather than forcing LF (Windows PowerShell handles both fine, and
# forcing LF here has no correctness benefit the way it does for POSIX
# shebangs).
*.ps1 text
